<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  CycloneDDS profile for the master host (cart AGX Orin) in the two-machine
  deployment. Pairs with orin.xml; see docs/design/multi_machine_deployment.md
  and docs/research/system/domain-split-link-pressure.md.

  TWO DOMAINS, TWO INTERFACES. This file used to be one <Domain Id="any"> bound
  to the LAN interface, which put every participant on this host on the wire:
  the whole Autoware graph discovered itself across the 100 Mb/s link, every
  writer with two or more readers chose the multicast locator and so sent its
  samples out of the NIC whether or not the orin wanted them, and one `ros2
  topic list` on the orin pulled the full endpoint set across. The link
  saturated in bursts and dropped. The split:

    * Domain 0 (the default; ROS_DOMAIN_ID unset) is bound to `lo`. It is the
      stack, exactly as loopback.xml runs it on a single machine. Nothing in it
      can reach the wire, because the interface it is bound to has no wire.

    * Domain ${GOLFCART_LINK_DOMAIN_ID} (42 unless config/runtime.conf says
      otherwise) is bound to the LAN address. Its only participant on this host
      is golfcart_domain_bridge, which copies the topics named in
      config/link/topics.yaml between the two. The orin's side is the mirror.

  The env reference in the Id attribute is expanded by CycloneDDS itself, so a
  bare `ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42 ros2 topic list` in a fresh shell lands in the right
  domain, and the default written here has to match the one in runtime.conf.

  The Id="any" section carries what both domains share. CycloneDDS merges it
  with whichever specific section matches, and a process started in any other
  domain id gets CycloneDDS defaults, which autodetermine an interface: that is
  the one way to put a stray participant on the wire, and it takes an explicit
  ROS_DOMAIN_ID to do it.

  Link-domain choices, and why:

    * The interface is selected by address, not by name: the NIC is named
      differently on the two boxes and can be renamed by NetworkManager, while
      the addresses are fixed by the router's DHCP reservations.
    * AllowMulticast is spdp, not default. With two participants on the link
      there is nothing for data multicast to save, and on a segment shared with
      the 4G router and other devices every multicast datagram is delivered to
      every port. Discovery still multicasts so the bridges find each other
      without configuration; data goes unicast to the one peer.
    * Peers pins the other host as a unicast discovery target anyway, as a
      fallback if multicast is ever blocked on the segment.
    * ParticipantIndex auto with a small maximum: only bridges and CLI tools
      live here. The internal domain keeps "none" (fully dynamic ports), as
      loopback.xml does, because nothing has to guess its ports there.

  This segment is the shared 4G LAN (192.168.125.0/24, master enP5p3s0 at
  100 Mb/s), not a dedicated link. Full-rate ZED image topics must not cross it
  and are not in topics.yaml; they are recorded on the orin's local disk.

  scripts/hardware/network/setup-interlink.sh installs a 1 Gb/s dedicated link on
  192.168.13.1/.2 over the free enP5p6s0 NIC. It is currently unused. Switching
  to it means changing the addresses below and in orin.xml, nothing else.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="https://cdds.io/config https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eclipse-cyclonedds/cyclonedds/master/etc/cyclonedds.xsd">

  <!-- The stack. Byte-for-byte what loopback.xml does on a single machine. -->
  <Domain Id="0">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="false" name="lo" priority="default" multicast="default" />
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>default</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>none</ParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>

  <!-- The link. One bridge participant, plus whatever CLI you point at it. -->
  <Domain Id="${GOLFCART_LINK_DOMAIN_ID:-42}">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="false" address="192.168.125.100" priority="default" multicast="default" />
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>spdp</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>20</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="192.168.125.101"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>

  <!-- Shared by both domains. -->
  <Domain Id="any">
    <General>
      <MaxMessageSize>65500B</MaxMessageSize>
    </General>
    <!--
      Iceoryx shared memory was removed on 2026-09-02. It never worked here: the
      runtime caps publisher ports at a count compiled into
      iceoryx_posh_deployment.hpp, this stack exceeds it, and the failure is a
      hard abort at participant creation rather than a fallback to the network
      transport. Cyclone registers a publisher port per writer even for types it
      cannot loan, so the cap is reached regardless of message type.

      Re-enabling it would mean rebuilding iceoryx from source with a raised
      IOX_MAX_PUBLISHERS, running iox-roudi as a service, and restoring the
      guard in scripts/env.sh. Nothing in this repo does any of that any more.
    -->
    <Internal>
      <!--
        Sized against a measured drop rate, not a guess. With the stack up,
        /proc/net/snmp showed 1308 UDP RcvbufErrors/s while lo carried 205k
        packets/s. Every one of those drops costs a NACK and a retransmit that
        CycloneDDS schedules on its "tev" thread, which `just profile perf`
        measured as the single hottest thread on the machine. Raising the
        socket buffer is what breaks that feedback loop.

        max= as well as min=: with only min= set, Cyclone asks for the size and
        accepts whatever the kernel gives back, so the request silently lands on
        net.core.rmem_default (208 kB here) unless the sysctl is also raised.
        See setup.sh cyclonedds-sysctl.
      -->
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="16MB" max="16MB"/>
      <!--
        The send side, and it was missing until 2026-09-21. Without this element
        Cyclone never calls setsockopt on a writer socket, so every one of them
        landed on net.core.wmem_default, which was still the 208 kB stock value
        while rmem_default had been raised to 16 MB.

        A 1.4 MB PointCloud2 fragments into roughly 22 datagrams at the
        MaxMessageSize above, and that burst does not fit 208 kB. Measured with
        both LiDARs live: the Velodyne driver's publishing thread sat in
        sock_alloc_send_pskb, the kernel's wait for send buffer space, on every
        scan. While parked there it stopped draining its own UDP receive socket,
        so RecvQ pinned at 13 to 16 MB and /proc/net/snmp showed 1304
        RcvbufErrors/s. The driver was idle at 0.5% CPU throughout: blocked, not
        busy, which is why CPU and GPU load both looked healthy. Velodyne output
        was 1.27 Hz and Falcon 2.91 Hz against 10 Hz hardware; the same driver
        run alone, with no stack around it, held 10.02 Hz.

        With 16 MB here both LiDARs hold 10 Hz, RecvQ stays 0 and RcvbufErrors
        stays 0/s.

        max= as well as min= for the same reason as the receive side: with only
        min= set Cyclone accepts whatever the kernel returns, so the request
        quietly falls back to wmem_default. The ceiling it needs is
        net.core.wmem_max, raised in setup.sh cyclonedds-sysctl.
      -->
      <SocketSendBufferSize min="16MB" max="16MB"/>
      <!--
        Cyclone's default heartbeat floor is 5 ms. Every reliable writer gets one,
        and each heartbeat draws AckNacks back from every matched reader, so the
        cost grows with the square of the participant count. 100 ms is still far
        inside the deadline of anything here - a lost sample is recovered on the
        next heartbeat, not this one - and it takes the bulk of the periodic work
        off tev.
      -->
      <HeartbeatInterval min="100ms" minsched="200ms" max="8s"/>
      <Watermarks>
        <WhcHigh>500kB</WhcHigh>
      </Watermarks>
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
